DECAF‑S: Provably Safe Multi‑Agent Coordination via Decentralized Equilibrium Learning with Adaptive Uncertainty Quantification

ouadi Maakoul 



Chapter 1

Problem Formulation and Mathematical Preliminaries

We consider a system of $n$ autonomous agents indexed by $[n] = \{1,\dots,n\}$. The agents operate in a common environment with state $s \in \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_s}$. Time is discrete, $t \in \mathbb{N}$. The state evolution is governed by a Markov transition kernel that depends on the mean‑field distribution of agent observation–action pairs. Formally, let

\mu_t = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n \delta_{(o_j^t, a_j^t)}

be the empirical mean‑field measure at time $t$, where $o_j^t$ is the local observation of agent $j$ and $a_j^t$ its action. The state transition satisfies

s_{t+1} \sim \mathcal{P}\bigl(\cdot \mid s_t, a_i^t, \mu_t\bigr),

Assumption A0 (No common noise). The transition kernel $\mathcal{P}$ depends on $\mu_t$ only through the deterministic aggregation of individual states; there is no aggregate shock that affects all agents simultaneously. Consequently, $\mu_t$ is a deterministic function of the joint policy profile, and the fixed‑point arguments of later sections are applicable.

Observations. Each agent $i$ receives

o_i^t = \mathcal{O}_i(s_t) + \epsilon_i^t,\qquad \|\epsilon_i^t\| \le \epsilon_{\max},

where $\{\epsilon_i^t\}$ is an arbitrary bounded (possibly adversarial) noise sequence. The observation function $\mathcal{O}_i$ is Lipschitz.

Actions. Agent $i$ chooses $a_i^t$ from a compact convex set $\mathcal{A}_i \subset \mathbb{R}^{d_a}$.

Communication. Agent $i$ may broadcast a message $m_i^t$ from a finite alphabet $\mathcal{M}$. Agent $j$ receives

m_{ij}^t =
\begin{cases}
m_i^{t-k}, & \text{with probability } 1-p_{\mathrm{corrupt}},\\[2pt]
\bot, & \text{with probability } p_{\mathrm{corrupt}},
\end{cases}

where the delay $k \ge 0$ is drawn from a known distribution with finite mean $\bar{k}$. The corruption events are independent across links.

Rewards. Agent $i$’s individual reward is $R_i(o_i, a_i, \mu) \in \mathbb{R}$, assumed to be $L_R$‑Lipschitz in $\mu$ under the Wasserstein‑1 distance $\mathcal{W}_1$, i.e.

|R_i(o_i, a_i, \mu) - R_i(o_i, a_i, \nu)| \le L_R \,\mathcal{W}_1(\mu, \nu).

Agents may have strictly conflicting objectives.

Safety. There exists a catastrophic set $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{unsafe}} \subset \mathcal{S}$. We require that for all $t$,

\mathbb{P}(s_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}) \le \delta,

for a prescribed tolerance $\delta \in (0,1)$.

Local information structure. Each agent $i$ selects its policy $\pi_i$ using only its local history $\mathcal{H}_i^t = \{o_i^{1:t}, m_{(\cdot)i}^{1:t}\}$. No central controller exists.

Mean‑field observability via communication (Assumption A1). Because the true $\mu_t$ is not directly observable from a single agent’s local observation, each agent reconstructs an estimate from the messages it receives. Let $\mathcal{N}_i$ be the set of agents whose messages are received by $i$ with non‑zero probability. For each $j \in \mathcal{N}_i$, the decoded message provides a reconstruction $(\hat{o}_j^t, \hat{a}_j^t)$. Then the estimated mean field is

\hat{\mu}_t^{(i)} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}_i|} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \delta_{(\hat{o}_j^t, \hat{a}_j^t)}.

We assume that, with high probability,

\mathcal{W}_1(\hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}, \mu_t) \le \epsilon_\mu,

where $\epsilon_\mu = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{|\mathcal{N}_i|})$ by concentration of measure. This bound is derived from standard empirical process arguments on the discrete approximation error of the codebook (Module 5) and the message corruption rate. All subsequent modules work with $\hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}$ in place of $\mu_t$, and theoretical guarantees carry an additive $\epsilon_\mu$‑dependent penalty.

Constrained mean‑field stochastic game. Each agent solves

\max_{\pi_i}\; \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^T \Bigl(R_i(o_i^t, a_i^t, \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}) - \lambda \,\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{conflict}}(\pi_i, \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)})\Bigr)\right]

subject to the local safety constraints specified in Chapter 4. The conflict penalty $\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{conflict}}$ is instantiated by the Pareto‑augmented reward in Chapter 3.

