Chapter 2

Module 1 – Iterative Conformal Belief Filter with BCLF Descent

Each agent maintains a belief $b_i^t$ over the state $s$. Because the full joint belief over $\mathcal{S}$ is intractable, we factorise it over a local dynamic Bayesian network. Let $\mathcal{N}_i$ denote the set of agents in the immediate neighbourhood whose messages affect agent $i$’s local dynamics. The belief factorises as

b_i^t(s) \propto \prod_{k \in \mathcal{N}_i} b_i^{(k)}(s^{(k)}),

where each factor $b_i^{(k)}$ is represented by a set of $K$ weighted particles $\{(s_\ell^{(k)}, w_\ell^{(k)})\}_{\ell=1}^K$.

2.1 Conformal message weighting

For every received message $m_{ji}^t$ with decoded value $\hat{y}_{ji}$, we assign a nonconformity score

S_{ji} = |\hat{y}_{ji} - f(x_{ji})|,

where $f$ is a predictive model trained on the calibration buffer, and $x_{ji}$ is the context (agent identifier, delay, etc.). The calibration buffer $\mathcal{D}_i = \{(x_\ell, y_\ell)\}_{\ell=1}^N$ contains the most recent $N$ (context, true noise) pairs. The conformal prediction set at level $\alpha$ is obtained from the empirical $(1-\alpha)$‑quantile:

q_{1-\alpha}^{(i)} = \inf\left\{ q : \frac{|\{\ell : S(x_\ell, y_\ell) \le q\}|}{N} \ge 1-\alpha \right\}.

The message weight combines delay discounting and conformal filtering:

w_{ji}^t = \exp(-\alpha_d \cdot k_{ji}) \cdot \mathbf{1}[S_{ji} \le q_{1-\alpha}^{(i)}].

Thus, only messages whose decoded value lies inside the conformal set are used for belief updating; the others are discarded ($w_{ji}^t = 0$). The same holds for corrupted messages ($m_{ji}^t = \bot$).

2.2 Iterative conformal prediction with covariate weighting

Endogenous distribution shift—agents adapting to the filter—breaks exact exchangeability. We employ iterative conformal prediction (ICP): every $\tau$ timesteps, the calibration window advances to the most recent $N$ samples and the quantile is recomputed using covariate‑weighted quantiles to account for the mismatch between the calibration and current context distributions.

Formally, let $p_t$ and $p_{\mathrm{cal}}$ denote the densities of the current and calibration contexts. We estimate the likelihood ratio $r(x) = p_t(x)/p_{\mathrm{cal}}(x)$ using a density ratio classifier, and define weights $v_\ell \propto r(x_\ell)$. The weighted quantile is then

q_{1-\alpha}^{(i)} = \text{weighted}\text{-quantile}( \{ (S_\ell, v_\ell) \}_{\ell=1}^N, 1-\alpha).

When the contexts are approximately exchangeable within each window, the total variation distance between the joint law of any permutation of the scores and the original sequence is bounded by $\eta_N$. Under standard assumptions, $\eta_N = \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\log N / N})$ (Barber et al., 2023).

2.3 Particle belief update

Agent $i$ performs a sequential importance resampling (SIR) step. Given the previous belief $b_i^t$ and the new observation $o_i^{t+1}$ and received messages, the posterior is proportional to

b_i^{t+1}(s) \propto \mathcal{O}_i(o_i^{t+1}\mid s) \int \mathcal{P}(s\mid s', a_i^t, \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}) \, b_i^t(s') \, ds' \cdot \prod_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} w_{ji}^t \cdot \operatorname{Dec}(m_{ji}^t).

The product over messages uses the conformal weights $w_{ji}^t$, so that only calibrated messages influence the belief. The particle representation is updated via standard SIR, with resampling triggered when the effective sample size $N_{\mathrm{eff}} = 1/\sum_k (w^{(k)})^2$ falls below $K/2$.

2.4 Belief Control Lyapunov Function (BCLF)

We define a Lyapunov function

V(b_i) = \mathrm{KL}\bigl(b_i \,\|\, P(s\mid\mathcal{H}_i)\bigr),

the Kullback–Leibler divergence to the true posterior. The calibrated update yields a contraction property.

Lemma 2.1 (BCLF Contraction). Under the conformal weighting scheme, there exist constants $\gamma \in (0,1)$ and $c > 0$ such that

\mathbb{E}_t\bigl[V(b_i^{t+1})\bigr] \le \gamma\, V(b_i^t) + c,

where $\mathbb{E}_t$ denotes conditional expectation given $\mathcal{F}_t$, and $c = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon_{\max}^2 + \epsilon_\mu)$.

Proof sketch. The Bayesian update with well‑calibrated likelihoods contracts the KL divergence towards the true posterior. The contraction factor $\gamma$ depends on the observation model’s Lipschitz constant and the signal‑to‑noise ratio. The additive constant $c$ arises from the residual uncertainty due to the observation noise bound $\epsilon_{\max}$ and the mean‑field estimation error $\epsilon_\mu$. ∎

2.5 Theorem 1 – Marginal coverage and BCLF descent

Theorem 2.2 (Marginal coverage with non‑exchangeability slack). Assume (i) within each calibration window of length $N$, the scores are approximately exchangeable with TV slack $\eta_N = \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\log N/N})$, (ii) scores are bounded by $B$, (iii) the likelihood ratio $r(x)$ is bounded by $C_v<\infty$. Then the ICP with covariate‑weighted quantiles satisfies

\liminf_{T\to\infty}\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{P}\bigl(y_t \in \mathcal{C}_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}\bigr) \ge 1-\alpha - \eta_N.

Consequently, the KL belief error enjoys the same marginal bound, and $V(b_i^t)$ converges exponentially to a neighbourhood of radius $c/(1-\gamma)$.

Proof. The weighted conformal predictor guarantees marginal coverage on each window with an additive slack $\eta_N$ due to the approximate exchangeability condition (see Barber et al., 2023). The average over time yields the long‑run property. Because the event $y_t \in \mathcal{C}_t$ coincides with the calibration of the likelihood weighting, the KL divergence is bounded by the same probability. The contraction inequality (Lemma 2.1) then implies

V(b_i^t) \le \gamma^t V(b_i^0) + \frac{c}{1-\gamma}.

∎

