Chapter 4

Module 3 – Multi‑Agent Belief Control Barrier Function with Equilibrium‑Aware Liveness Fallback

Safety is enforced through a Belief Control Barrier Function (BCBF) that ensures the state never enters $\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}$ with high probability.

4.1 Local BCBF quadratic program

Each agent maintains a function $h_i: \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S}) \to \mathbb{R}$ such that $h_i(b_i) \ge 0$ indicates safety. The discrete‑time BCBF condition is

\Delta h_i(b_i, a) := \mathbb{E}_{s'\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot|b_i,a)}\bigl[h_i(b_i')\bigr] - h_i(b_i) \ge -\gamma\, h_i(b_i),

for a chosen $\gamma \in (0,1)$. To enforce this, agent $i$ solves the quadratic program with a slack variable $\xi_i \ge 0$:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{a,\xi\ge0} \quad & \|a - a_i^{\mathrm{raw}}\|^2 + M \xi \\
\text{s.t.}\quad & \Delta h_i(b_i^t, a) + \gamma h_i(b_i^t) \ge -\xi,\quad \forall s \in \operatorname{supp}(b_i^t).
\end{aligned}

The slack variable absorbs infeasibility in dense scenarios (see discussion below). When $\xi_i = 0$, the safety constraint is strictly satisfied.

4.2 Safety confirmation protocol

1. Agent $i$ broadcasts its candidate $a_i^{\mathrm{safe}}$ and the support of $b_i^t$ to its safety‑critical neighbours $\mathcal{N}_i^{\mathrm{safe}}$.
2. It waits at most $\tau_{\mathrm{safe}} = 2\bar{k}$ timesteps.
3. Each neighbour $j$ checks whether $a_i^{\mathrm{safe}}$ would violate its own BCBF: it verifies $\Delta h_j(b_j^t, a_i^{\mathrm{safe}}) + \gamma h_j(b_j^t) \ge 0$ over its belief support, and sends $\text{Confirm}_{ji}=1$ if true, else $0$.
4. If all neighbours respond with $1$ before timeout and $\xi_i=0$, agent $i$ executes $a_i^{\mathrm{safe}}$.
5. Otherwise, agent $i$ enters the priority liveness fallback.

4.3 Priority liveness fallback – social mini‑game

Agents in conflict form a local normal‑form game with payoff

U_i(a_i, a_{-i}^{\mathcal{N}}) = -\max_{j} \max_{s\in\operatorname{supp}(b_j^t)} \bigl[-\Delta h_j(b_j^t, a_i) - \gamma h_j(b_j^t)\bigr]_+ - \kappa\, \text{CollisionCost}(a_i, a_{-i}^{\mathcal{N}}),

where $[x]_+ = \max(0,x)$. The priority order is determined by the Pareto weight $w_i$: agents with smaller $w_i$ (greater deficit) have higher priority. The unique mixed‑strategy Nash equilibrium of this zero‑sum‑like game (with priority tie‑breaking) is computed by iterated best response. This equilibrium serves as the fallback action.

4.4 Theorem 3 – Deadlock‑free joint safety

Theorem 4.1 (Joint safety with density‑dependent guarantee). Assume (i) all local BCBFs are mutually compatible (the intersection of safe sets is non‑empty), (ii) the agent density $\rho \le \rho^*$, where $\rho^*$ is a critical threshold depending on the geometry and dynamics, (iii) the BCBF‑QP (with slack) is strictly feasible ($\xi_i = 0$) for all agents when $\rho\le\rho^*$, (iv) CBF functions are Lipschitz. Then

1. Safety: $\mathbb{P}(s_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{unsafe}}) \le \delta$ for all $t$,
2. Deadlock freedom: There exists a finite $T_{\max}$ such that every agent eventually acts; no agent blocks forever.

Proof sketch. (Safety) Under $\rho\le\rho^*$ and compatibility, the BCBF‑QP is strictly feasible, so $\xi_i=0$. The executed action satisfies the BCBF condition, which is a Lyapunov function for the safe set extended to belief space. By Theorem 1, the true state lies in the support of $b_i^t$ with probability at least $1-\alpha-\eta_N$. On this event, the BCBF condition ensures forward invariance, so the state remains safe. With $\alpha$ and $\eta_N$ chosen such that $\alpha+\eta_N \le \delta$, we obtain the safety probability bound. (Deadlock freedom) The priority order $w_i$ induces a total order; the highest‑priority agent can always act without requiring confirmation. Its action reduces a conflict potential function, guaranteeing finite‑time resolution. ∎

Remark on high density. When $\rho > \rho^*$, the slack may be strictly positive, softening the constraint. Safety is then probabilistic, with violation probability bounded by $M^{-1}\xi_i^*$. The dissertation will quantify $\rho^*$ for example scenarios.

