Chapter 5

Module 4 – Stage‑Wise Optimistic Online Mirror Descent and Convergence

We now turn to the learning algorithm that drives agents toward an equilibrium of the Pareto‑augmented mean‑field game.

5.1 Euclidean optimistic OMD update

Each agent maintains a policy parameter $\pi_i$ in a compact convex set $\Pi_i$. At each timestep, after taking action $a_i^t = \pi_i(b_i^t, \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)})$, the agent constructs a loss vector

\ell_i^t = -\nabla_{\pi_i} \tilde{R}_i\bigl(o_i^t, \pi_i(b_i^t, \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}), \hat{\mu}_t^{(i)}\bigr).

The optimistic bonus is $m_i^t = \ell_i^t - \ell_i^{t-1}$. The update is

\pi_i^{t+1} = \operatorname{Proj}_{\Pi_i}\!\Bigl(\pi_i^t - \eta_t (\ell_i^t + m_i^t)\Bigr),

where $\operatorname{Proj}_{\Pi_i}$ is the Euclidean projection, and the step size follows the adaptive schedule

\eta_t = \frac{\eta_0}{\sqrt{\sum_{s=1}^t \|\ell_i^s + m_i^s\|^2}}.

Stages of length $L$ are used; the average policy over a stage $\bar{\pi}_i^k = \frac{1}{L}\sum_{t\in \text{stage } k} \pi_i^t$ is tracked.

5.2 Non‑stationarity handling

To cope with slowly varying environment dynamics, the regulariser is re‑centered every $W$ steps:

\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{new}}(\pi) = \frac{1}{2}\|\pi - \pi_i^t\|_2^2 + \kappa \, \mathrm{KL}(\pi \| \pi_i^t),

where $\kappa$ is proportional to the estimated rate of environment change. The KL term provides controlled forgetting.

5.3 Convergence to correlated equilibrium

The average‑iterate convergence relies on establishing a Regret bounded by Variation in Utilities (RVU) property for the Euclidean OOMD update in the mean‑field stochastic game setting.

Definition 5.1 (RVU property). An online learning algorithm enjoys RVU if for any sequence of utilities $u_t$, the cumulative regret $R_T = \max_{\pi^*}\sum_t \langle \ell_t, \pi_t - \pi^*\rangle$ satisfies

R_T \le \alpha + \beta \sum_{t=1}^T \|\ell_t - \ell_{t-1}\|^2 + \gamma \sum_{t=1}^T \|\pi_t - \pi_{t-1}\|^2,

with constants $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ independent of $T$.

Lemma 5.2 (RVU for Euclidean OOMD). For the step‑size schedule above and the Euclidean regulariser, the optimistic OMD update satisfies an RVU bound with $\alpha = \mathcal{O}(\log T)$ and $\beta,\gamma$ constants depending on the game.

Proof sketch. By standard arguments, the optimistic term cancels the main part of the variation, leaving a bound proportional to the squared variation of losses and policies. The adaptive step size ensures the cumulative term is $\mathcal{O}(\log T)$. ∎

Theorem 5.3 (Average‑iterate CCE convergence). Suppose the mean‑field game is approximated with error $\epsilon_{\mathrm{MF}} = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{n} + \epsilon_\mu)$. Then the time‑average policy profile $\bar{\pi} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T \pi_i^t$ converges to an $\epsilon$‑correlated coarse equilibrium with

\epsilon \le C \frac{\log T}{T} \bigl(1 + \epsilon_{\mathrm{MF}}\bigr),

where $C$ is a game‑dependent constant. Moreover, we establish that this rate holds for Euclidean OOMD specifically—a novel result extending the OFTRL‑based analysis of Yorulmaz et al. (2025).

Proof. From the RVU property, the average regret of each agent is $\mathcal{O}(\log T / T)$. By the folk theorem for online learning in games, the empirical distribution of play converges to the set of correlated equilibria at that rate. The mean‑field approximation error adds a constant factor $(1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{MF}})$. The technical contribution is the proof that the Euclidean OOMD update satisfies the RVU property needed for the regret bound, despite the absence of the smoothness structure used in the OFTRL analysis. Details will appear in the dissertation. ∎

Open Problem (last‑iterate convergence). The asymptotic behaviour of the last iterate $\pi_i^T$ in general‑sum mean‑field games remains an open question. The dissertation will explore whether the Euclidean OOMD with forgetting achieves last‑iterate convergence at a comparable rate.

