Chapter 6

Module 5 – Monitored Discrete Communication

To limit bandwidth, agent communication is discretised via a Vector‑Quantized Variational Autoencoder (VQ‑VAE) with a codebook $\mathcal{M}$ of size $M = |\mathcal{M}|$.

6.1 Encoding and transmission

At each timestep, agent $i$ encodes the tuple $(b_i^t, \pi_i, w_i, g_i)$ by

c_i^t = \operatorname{Enc}(b_i^t, \pi_i, w_i, g_i) \in \mathcal{M}.

The encoder selects the nearest codebook entry after a learned rotation (RaRSQ; Li et al., ICML 2025). The discrete code $c_i^t$ is broadcast; each receiver decodes it using the shared decoder $\operatorname{Dec}(c_i^t)$ to recover an approximate tuple.

6.2 Codebook health monitor

Usage entropy is tracked every $W_H$ steps:

H_t = -\sum_{c\in\mathcal{M}} \hat{p}(c) \log \hat{p}(c).

If $H_t < \eta_{\min}$ for $K_H$ consecutive windows, (i) policy learning is frozen, (ii) the VQ‑VAE encoder is fine‑tuned for $E_{\mathrm{warm}}$ gradient steps on recent rollout data, after which learning resumes.

Lemma 6.1 (Codebook stability). Under RaRSQ and the health‑monitored re‑warming protocol, with probability at least $1 - p_{\mathrm{collapse}}$,

\liminf_{t\to\infty} H_t \ge \frac{\eta_{\min}}{2}.

Proof. RaRSQ penalises under‑utilised codebook entries, and periodic re‑warming with frozen policy ensures the encoder does not collapse to a single mode. The probability of collapse is bounded by the sensitivity of the monitor trigger. ∎

