Chapter 7

Module 6 – Counterfactual Collusion Detection

Agents may collude by exchanging messages that strategically misrepresent information. We design a statistical test based on Shapley values to detect abnormal message dependencies.

7.1 Shapley Message Selector approximation

For a pair $(i,j)$, the marginal contribution of agent $i$’s messages to agent $j$’s reward is approximated by a Shapley Message Selector (SMS) network (Xue et al., IJCAI 2022):

\phi_{ij} = \operatorname{SMS}_{\theta}(o_j^t).

If $\phi_{ij}$ exceeds a threshold $\phi_{\min}$, a full hypothesis test is triggered.

7.2 Hypothesis test and sample complexity

Independent episodes are collected in two conditions: real messages from $i$ and counterfactual episodes where $i$’s messages are replaced by uniform codebook noise. Let $\Delta R_j = R_j^{\mathrm{real}} - R_j^{\mathrm{cf}}$. We test

H_0: \mathbb{E}[\Delta R_j] \ge \delta_0 \quad\text{(robust cooperation)},


H_1: \mathbb{E}[\Delta R_j] < \delta_0 \quad\text{(possible collusion)}.

Using a one‑sided two‑sample $t$‑test at level $\alpha_{\mathrm{det}}$ to detect an effect size $\epsilon_{\mathrm{det}} = \delta_0 - \delta_1$ with power $1-\beta_{\mathrm{det}}$, the required number of independent episode pairs (real vs counterfactual) is

N_{\mathrm{det}} \ge \frac{2\sigma^2\bigl(\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha_{\mathrm{det}}/2) + \Phi^{-1}(1-\beta_{\mathrm{det}})\bigr)^2}{\epsilon_{\mathrm{det}}^2},

where $\sigma^2 = \sigma_{\mathrm{real}}^2 + \sigma_{\mathrm{cf}}^2$ is estimated from pilot data. Upon detecting collusion, agent $j$ down‑weights $i$’s future messages.

