Chapter 8

The Equilibrium–Safety Coincidence Conjecture

The central theoretical contribution of this dissertation is the conjecture that the Pareto‑augmented MF‑NE and the social mini‑game fallback are not independent constructs; they coincide under appropriate conditions.

Conjecture 8.1 (Equilibrium–Safety Coincidence). Let $\mu^*$ be a stationary MF‑NE of the smoothed Pareto game (Theorem 3.2) with associated priority weights $\{w_i^*\}$. Assume the agent density satisfies $\rho \le \rho^*$ so that the BCBF‑QP is strictly feasible (Lemma 8.2 below). Then the mixed‑strategy Nash equilibrium of the social mini‑game (Section 4.3) parameterised by the same weights equals the restriction of the global MF‑NE policy $\pi_i^*$ to the safe action space $\mathcal{A}_i^{\mathrm{safe}}(b_i)$.

Planned proof – three lemmas.

Lemma 8.2 (Feasibility). For all beliefs $b_i$ in the support of $\mu^*$, the safe action set $\mathcal{A}_i^{\mathrm{safe}}(b_i) = \{a \in \mathcal{A}_i : \Delta h_i(b_i,a) + \gamma h_i(b_i) \ge 0\ \forall s\in\operatorname{supp}(b_i)\}$ is non‑empty.

Proof strategy. Use the Nagumo invariance condition: at every point of the boundary of the safe set, there exists a feasible velocity. With a sufficiently fine particle discretisation (error $\mathcal{O}(1/K)$), the discrete‑time constraint inherits feasibility. ∎

Lemma 8.3 (Restriction). The policy $\pi_i^*$ restricted to $\mathcal{A}_i^{\mathrm{safe}}(b_i)$ forms a Nash equilibrium of the BCBF‑restricted game.

Proof strategy. Apply the KKT conditions to the best‑response problem. Because the augmented reward is affine in $a_i$ and the safe action set is convex, the restricted best response is the Euclidean projection of $\pi_i^*$ onto $\mathcal{A}_i^{\mathrm{safe}}$. Show that this projection remains a best response when all agents play the same projection. ∎

Lemma 8.4 (Coincidence via KKT duality). When the BCBF‑QP is strictly feasible (Lemma 8.2), strong duality holds. The mini‑game payoff $U_i$ is the KKT dual function of the BCBF‑QP objective evaluated at Lagrange multipliers equal to the Pareto weights $w_i^*$.

Proof strategy. Write the Lagrangian $\mathcal{L}(a,\lambda) = \|a-a^{\mathrm{raw}}\|^2 - \lambda (\Delta h_i + \gamma h_i)$. The dual function is $g(\lambda) = \min_a \mathcal{L}(a,\lambda)$. The social mini‑game payoff is shown to equal $-g(w_i^*)$, hence the Nash equilibrium of the mini‑game coincides with the primal‑dual optimum, which by Lemma 8.3 is the restricted MF‑NE. ∎

The proof of these lemmas constitutes the main technical chapters of the dissertation. Empirical validation on an intersection management simulator will precede the formal proof to provide supporting evidence.

